nvironment shifting several points could pull this into competitive territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Michael Baumgartner (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Eastern Washington's 5th District leans Republican by about five points, and first-term incumbent Michael Baumgartner is seeking a second term against Democrat Carmela Conroy in a rematch. Baumgartner holds a decisive financial advantage, having raised roughly four times as much as Conroy and entering the fall with about $954,000 on hand. Outside groups have spent modestly against him, but little on his behalf.\n\nMost expert raters call the race Safe R, with a couple rating it Likely R. The only available polls, conducted in spring by Democratic-leaning firms, showed Baumgartner ahead by about six points among likely voters. Prediction markets give Conroy roughly a one-in-five chance. The forecast expects Baumgartner to win by a solid but not overwhelming margin, with uncertainty wide because recent public polling is thin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0968, "margin": -8.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1087, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0951, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0945, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1116, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0732, "margin": -9.0}]}